second artist, birth date for a different person, or second sport is evidence to retain it. Check each proposed deletion once more against all originals before producing the final short list.\nInput: {\"question\": \"When was Justice Cole born?\", \"slot_hint\": \"birth_date\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 108, \"text\": \"Charles Cleaves Cole Charles Cleaves Cole (May 22, 1841 – March 17, 1905) was a United States federal judge.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d00\", \"start\": 109, \"end\": 183, \"text\": \"Born in Hiram, Maine, Cole read law to enter the bar in 1866, and an LL.B.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d00\", \"start\": 184, \"end\": 216, \"text\": \"from Harvard Law School in 1867.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d00\", \"start\": 217, \"end\": 345, \"text\": \"He was a Private in the United States Army during the American Civil War, serving in the 17th Maine Infantry, from 1862 to 1865.\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d00\", \"start\": 346, \"end\": 507, \"text\": \"He was in private practice in Portland, Maine from 1866 to 1867, in West Union, West Virginia from 1868 to 1870, in Parkersburg, West Virginia from 1870 to 1874.\"}, \"D1S6\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d00\", \"start\": 508, \"end\": 540, \"text\": \"He was a Prosecuting attorney of\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 90, \"text\": \"Charles Cleaves Cole (January 1, 1900 – March 17, 1905) was a United States federal judge.\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d01\", \"start\": 91, \"end\": 165, \"text\": \"Born in Hiram, Maine, Cole read law to enter the bar in 1866, and an LL.B.\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d01\", \"start\": 166, \"end\": 198, \"text\": \"from Harvard Law School in 1867.\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d01\", \"start\": 199, \"end\": 327, \"text\": \"He was a Private in the United States Army during the American Civil War, serving in the 17th Maine Infantry, from 1862 to 1865.\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d01\", \"start\": 328, \"end\": 489, \"text\": \"He was in private practice in Portland, Maine from 1866 to 1867, in West Union, West Virginia from 1868 to 1870, in Parkersburg, West Virginia from 1870 to 1874.\"}, \"D2S6\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d01\", \"start\": 490, \"end\": 568, \"text\": \"He was a Prosecuting attorney of Wood County, West Virginia from 1874 to 1878.\"}, \"D3S1\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 9, \"text\": \"Luther F.\"}, \"D3S2\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02\", \"start\": 10, \"end\": 180, \"text\": \"Cole Luther Francis Cole (October 25, 1925 – July 26, 2013) was a lawyer and politician from Baton Rouge, Louisiana, who served as a state legislator and then as a judge.\"}, \"D3S3\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02\", \"start\": 181, \"end\": 281, \"text\": \"Cole was born in Alexandria in Rapides Parish, one of twelve children of Clement and Catherine Cole.\"}, \"D3S4\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02\", \"start\": 282, \"end\": 376, \"text\": \"He graduated from Bolton High School and served in the United States Navy during World War II.\"}, \"D3S5\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02\", \"start\": 377, \"end\": 497, \"text\": \"He attended Louisiana Tech University in Ruston and graduated from Louisiana State University Law Center in Baton Rouge.\"}, \"D3S6\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02\", \"start\": 498, \"end\": 576, \"text\": \"He joined a friend, Warren Mengis, in the formation of the Cole and Mengis law\"}, \"D4S1\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 147, \"text\": \"him by more than three decades, dying in Providence on 21 June 1811 in her 87th year; she was buried in Saint John's Church Cemetery in Providence.\"}, \"D4S2\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03\", \"start\": 148, \"end\": 193, \"text\": \"Cole's immediate ancestry is found in John O.\"}, \"D4S3\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03\", \"start\": 194, \"end\": 243, \"text\": \"Austin's Genealogical Dictionary of Rhode Island.\"}, \"D4S4\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03\", \"start\": 244, \"end\": 318, \"text\": \"The ancestry of Cole's grandmother, Susanna Cole, was published by John D.\"}, \"D4S5\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03\", \"start\": 319, \"end\": 345, \"text\": \"Champlin in 1913 and 1914.\"}, \"D4S6\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03\", \"start\": 346, \"end\": 522, \"text\": \"The Dexter ancestry of Cole's mother is from the Dexter genealogy (1905), supplemented with more recent scholarship from \\\"The Great Migration\\\" series as cited within the chart.\"}, \"D4S7\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03\", \"start\": 523, \"end\": 612, \"text\": \"\\\"Online sources\\\" John Cole (judge) John Cole (1715–1777) was a lawyer who became the 12th\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"Justice Cole\", \"scope\": \"birth date\", \"claim\": \"Justice Cole was born on May 22, 1841.\", \"answer\": \"May 22, 1841\", \"quote\": \"Charles Cleaves Cole Charles Cleaves Cole (May 22, 1841 – March 17, 1905) was a United States federal judge.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"Justice Cole\", \"scope\": \"birth date\", \"claim\": \"Justice Cole was born on January 1, 1900.\", \"answer\": \"January 1, 1900\", \"quote\": \"Charles Cleaves Cole (January 1, 1900 – March 17, 1905) was a United States federal judge.\", \"original_document_number\": 2}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02:c0\", \"entity\": \"Justice Cole\", \"scope\": \"birth date\", \"claim\": \"Justice Cole was born on October 25, 1925.\", \"answer\": \"October 25, 1925\", \"quote\": \"Cole Luther Francis Cole (October 25, 1925 – July 26, 2013) was a lawyer and politician from Baton Rouge, Louisiana, who served as a state legislator and then as a judge.\", \"original_document_number\": 3}], \"R5_answers\": [\"May 22, 1841\", \"January 1, 1900\", \"October 25, 1925\"], \"candidates\": [{\"id\": \"C0\", \"answer\": \"May 22, 1841\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C1\", \"answer\": \"January 1, 1900\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C2\", \"answer\": \"October 25, 1925\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}]}"
